# Contributing to {{ project_name }}

This project follows [trellis](https://github.com/265866/trellis): strict lints, a structure
checker, and pull requests that keep rules and code apart. [docs/style.md](docs/style.md) lists
every style rule, what checks it, and the review checklist for the rest;
[AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md) holds the rules no check decides. Both apply to people and coding agents
alike.

## Set up

Install [rustup](https://rustup.rs/), [cargo-binstall](https://github.com/cargo-bins/cargo-binstall),
[uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/), [Go](https://go.dev/dl/) for actionlint, and
[shellcheck](https://www.shellcheck.net/) 0.11.0, which actionlint runs on the workflows' scripts.
{%- if kind != 'service' and 'windows' in platforms %} On
Windows, also install the [Visual Studio Build Tools](https://visualstudio.microsoft.com/visual-cpp-build-tools/)
with the "Desktop development with C++" workload, which Rust's MSVC toolchain links with.
{%- endif %}
Then install the tools the checks use:

```sh
cargo binstall just@1.59.0 cargo-deny@0.20.2 cargo-shear@1.14.0 cargo-nextest@0.9.150 \
  cargo-dylint@6.3.0 dylint-link@6.3.0 typos-cli@1.51.1 taplo-cli@0.10.0 lychee@0.24.2
{%- if kind != 'library' %}
cargo binstall cargo-about@0.9.3
{%- endif %}
go install github.com/rhysd/actionlint/cmd/actionlint@v1.7.12
uv tool install zizmor@1.31.1
just install-toolchains
```

These are the versions CI pins in `.github/workflows/`.

`just install-toolchains` installs the stable toolchain from `rust-toolchain.toml`, the nightly
rustfmt, and the nightly the custom lints build on.

### Editors

The options in `rustfmt.toml` exist only in nightly rustfmt. For rust-analyzer, set
`rust-analyzer.rustfmt.extraArgs` to `["+nightly-2026-10-28"]`, so format-on-save matches
`just fmt-check`.

## Check a change

`just ci` runs every check CI runs. `just` alone lists them, so you can run one at a time:

| Recipe | Checks |
| --- | --- |
| `just fmt-check` | Rust and TOML formatting. `just fmt` fixes both. |
| `just typos` | Spelling. |
| `just actionlint`, `just zizmor` | The GitHub workflows, and their security. |
| `just shear` | Every declared dependency is used. |
| `just deny` | Advisories, licenses, banned crates and sources (`deny.toml`). |
| `just links` | Every relative link and anchor in the Markdown files. |
| `just policy` | The structure rules: workspace lints, manifests, crate dependencies, the release changelog, the docs index. |
| `just lint` | clippy on every target, warnings as errors. |
| `just dylint` | The custom lints from trellis, warnings as errors. |
| `just test` | The tests through nextest, then the doctests. |
| `just docs` | The docs, private items included, warnings as errors. |
| `just build-release` | The release build. |
{%- if kind != 'library' %}
| `just notices` | The license notices a release ships, in `target/THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.html`. It fails on a license `about.toml` doesn't accept. |
{%- endif %}
{%- if kind == 'library' and publish_crate %}
| `just package` | The crate's package holds the README and its license files. |
{%- endif %}
| `just rule-changes [base]` | Rules and code change in separate pull requests. |

To silence a lint, put an `expect` with a reason on the smallest item it applies to:

- a clippy or rustc lint: `#[expect(clippy::lint_name, reason = "...")]`;
- a custom lint: `#[cfg_attr(dylint_lib = "trellis_lints", expect(lint_name, reason = "..."))]`.

Nothing else silences a lint: no `allow`, no inner `#![...]` attributes, and no reason shorter than
20 characters. An `expect` fails once its lint stops firing, so a stale exception doesn't linger.

### Tests

Put a test next to the code it tests, or in the crate's `tests/` directory when it uses only the
crate's public API. A test is never skipped or retried: `#[ignore]` fails `just dylint`, and
nextest has no retries.

### Advisories

The Advisories workflow also runs `just deny` every week. A new advisory can fail a pull request
that changed nothing; fix it with `cargo update -p <crate>` in its own pull request.

## Send a change

Pull requests are squash-merged, so the title becomes the commit on `main`. It must be a
[conventional commit](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/), such as `feat(cli): add a --json flag`
or `fix(config)!: reject unknown keys`. A `!` marks a breaking change.

A pull request changes either the rules or the code, never both. When a new rule would flag
existing code, either fix the code in one pull request and add the rule in the next, or add the
rule together with an `expect` and a real reason at each place it flags. `just rule-changes` sorts
the changed files:

- **Neither:** the root `README.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`, `SECURITY.md`, `LICENSE*`, `.editorconfig` and
  `Cargo.lock`, the `LICENSE*` files in a crate's directory, the Markdown files in `docs/` other
  than `docs/style.md` and agent instruction files, and a `.rs` change that only adds or removes
  `expect` attributes.
- **Code:** every other file under `crates/` and every `.rs` file, apart from the rule files
  below. A crate's `Cargo.toml` is code, but the part of a change that touches its
  `[build-dependencies]` or its `build` key is a rule, so a change to both is both. A proc-macro
  dependency is code: list the crate in `proc_macro_crates` in one pull request, then add the
  dependency and its first use in the next.
- **Rules:** everything else, including the root `Cargo.toml` outside `[workspace.dependencies]`
  and `[patch]`, the tool configuration files, the `justfile`, build scripts, `.github/`,
  `.copier-answers.yml`, this file, `docs/style.md` and `AGENTS.md`. A file the checker doesn't
  know{{ ', such as `Containerfile`,' if kind == 'service' else '' }} is a rule too.

## Set up once per repository

{% if kind != 'library' -%}
- Keep the repository public. On a personal account, GitHub attests release files only in public
  repositories, and the release workflow stops when it can't attest.
{% endif -%}
- In Settings, General, allow squash merging only, with the pull request title as its default
  message, so the title becomes the commit on `main`.
- Install the [Renovate app](https://github.com/apps/renovate) on the repository. Each week it opens
  one pull request with the crate updates other than major ones, and one for each major update.
  The toolchain, tool, action and image pins arrive with trellis updates.
- Allow the rule-change check to run. From 2026-12-02 GitHub blocks `pull_request_target` in
  public repositories unless an Actions policy allows it, and without it no pull request gets the
  "Rule changes stand alone" status:

  ```sh
  gh api --method POST repos/{{ github_owner }}/{{ project_name }}/actions/policies --input - <<'EOF'
  {"name": "Rule-change check", "enforcement": "active",
   "conditions": {"workflow_path": {"include": [".github/workflows/rule-changes.yml"], "exclude": []}},
   "rules": [{"type": "restrict_action_events",
              "parameters": {"allowed_events": ["pull_request_target"]}}]}
  EOF
  ```

- Create the label release-plz puts on its PR: `gh label create release`.
- Add a fine-grained token with contents and pull request write access to this repository as the
  `RELEASE_PLZ_TOKEN` secret. A pull request that `GITHUB_TOKEN` opens starts no
  `pull_request_target` workflow, so without the token the release PR never gets the
  "Rule changes stand alone" status and can't merge.
- Turn on private vulnerability reporting, which [SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md) points to.
{%- if kind == 'library' and publish_crate %}
- Publish the first version by hand with `cargo publish`. If that commit has no `vX.Y.Z` tag, tag it
  with its version and push the tag, so release-plz starts from it. Then add this repository's
  `release.yml` as a trusted publisher in the crate's settings on crates.io. Later releases publish
  through it. Every crate you add is published too, so do the same for each one.
{%- endif %}
{%- if kind == 'service' %}
- After the first release, make the `{{ project_name }}` package public in its package settings on
  GitHub, so others can pull the image.
{%- endif %}
{%- set os_list = ['linux'] if kind == 'service' else platforms %}
{%- set job_names = {'linux': 'Ubuntu', 'macos': 'macOS', 'windows': 'Windows'} %}
- After the first pull request's checks run (GitHub offers only checks it has seen), protect
  `main` and require these checks: `PR title`, `Quick checks`,
  {% for os in os_list %}`{{ job_names[os] }}`, {% endfor %}
{%- if kind == 'service' %}`Container image`, {% endif %}and the "Rule changes stand alone"
  status with GitHub Actions as its source.
{%- if kind == 'binary' %} The `Release build` checks run only when a pull request
  changes what goes into a release, so don't require them.
{%- endif %}
{%- if kind == 'service' %}
  `Container image` builds the image, as `just image` does, and runs it with `--version`, so the
  service must answer `--version` and exit 0.
{%- endif %}

## Releases

release-plz keeps a release PR open with the next version and its `CHANGELOG.md` entry. Merging it
releases that version, with that entry as the release notes;
[release.yml](.github/workflows/release.yml) does the rest. Before 1.0, a `feat` or a `!` bumps the
minor version and a `fix` the patch version. To ship:

1. To reword an entry, edit `CHANGELOG.md` on the release PR's branch, as the last step before you
   merge: the next merge to `main` closes the release PR and opens a new one without the edit.
   The first release after you add a crate comes out as a patch when only that crate changed. For a
   minor one, change the version on that branch too: in the root `Cargo.toml`, in `Cargo.lock` and
   in the `CHANGELOG.md` heading. Its notes can repeat older entries; delete those. Renaming the
   main crate stops the release workflow with an error that says what to do by hand.
2. Merge it once its checks pass. Merge no other PR until the release workflow's
   **{{ 'Release' if kind == 'library' else 'Tag' }}** job finishes, or release-plz may propose the same version again.
{%- if kind != 'library' %}
3. If a later job fails, re-run the failed jobs. They pick up from the same tag.
{%- endif %}
{%- if kind != 'library' %}

